{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 34, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering move to decriminalize personal drug use is ending as a new statute reinstates criminal penalties for possessing small quantities of substances like fentanyl, heroin, and meth. Under HB4002, individuals caught with these drugs can either face a misdemeanor charge—potentially leading to six months in jail—or opt into a health-focused pathway involving behavioral interventions and diversion efforts. The law shifts focus toward public safety, particularly near areas like playgrounds, where penalties for distribution are now stricter.\n\n***\n\nThe legislation offers local jurisdictions flexibility to design their own support systems, though participation isn’t required. Supporters argue this approach allows regions to tailor responses to their capacity, while critics warn it may lead to inconsistent outcomes across regions. Police are instructed to offer an alternative to arrest only if specific conditions are met: no outstanding warrants, no violent conduct, no additional charges, and medical stability. If these criteria are satisfied, officers contact a dedicated line to connect the individual with a mobile health team.\n\n***\n\nTime is critical: if a health professional can’t arrive within half an hour, the person will be taken into custody. Officers cannot delay enforcement while waiting, meaning the window for intervention is narrow and logistically demanding. This creates pressure on local health services to respond quickly and efficiently, with many wondering whether resources are sufficient to meet demand.\n\n***\n\nThe shift reflects a broader national conversation about how to address substance use without relying solely on punishment. As the country reevaluates its approach, some wonder whether compassion or control will guide policy. One might say that even the simplest acts—like eating a meal or shopping for groceries—can feel out of reach when systems fail to support those in crisis. And sometimes, the only thing left to say is that the tongue cannot speak the truth when the system is too rigid to listen.\n\nP.P.S Age, country, eat, shopping, tongue", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.00015507926582358778}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.005780527368187904}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.13278159499168396}, {"token": " move", "logprob": -0.10517826676368713}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -8.964136941358447e-05}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": -0.21991808712482452}, {"token": "riminal", "logprob": -0.004677188582718372}, {"token": "ize", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " personal", "logprob": -0.554117739200592}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -8.904537389753386e-05}, {"token": " use", "logprob": -0.31348296999931335}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.007826023735105991}, {"token": " ending", "logprob": -0.24009528756141663}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -0.9124716520309448}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.03640199452638626}, {"token": " new", "logprob": -0.03628002479672432}, {"token": " statute", "logprob": -0.050031621009111404}, {"token": " reinst", "logprob": -1.1101841926574707}, {"token": "ates", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": " criminal", "logprob": -1.133376121520996}, {"token": " penalties", "logprob": -0.012502036988735199}, {"token": " for", "logprob": -1.1920922133867862e-06}, {"token": " possessing", "logprob": -0.6763035655021667}, {"token": " small", "logprob": -0.001211862312629819}, {"token": " quantities", "logprob": -0.10060681402683258}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " substances", "logprob": -0.0386304147541523}, {"token": " like", "logprob": -0.0002317160106031224}, {"token": " f", "logprob": -0.0027789566665887833}, {"token": "entanyl", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.0002613918040879071}, {"token": " 